# The things in a pencil case on the shelf's art tools, with do you have and I do not have.
lesson language-pencil-case v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=3 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "In my pencil case"
  goal "Name what is in a pencil case in [[language.name]], ask whether someone has one, and answer yes or no."

  look {
    scene 23x7 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        arttools case tools=["pencil", "crayon", "marker", "brush", "eraser"] colours=["blue", "red", "green", "yellow", "red"] layout=row ring=-1
      }
    }
    say """
      In Ana's pencil case there is [[school.the-pencil]], [[school.the-crayon]],
      [[school.the-felt-pen]], [[school.the-brush]] and [[school.the-rubber]]. To ask for one, say
      [[school.do-you-have-a-pencil]], do you have a pencil? The answer is [[school.yes-i-have]],
      yes, I have, or [[school.no-i-do-not-have]], no, I do not.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.pencil-case-name k=2
    }
    show language.pencil-case-name k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.pencil-case-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.pencil-case-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.pencil-case-name k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.pencil-case-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.pencil-case-write k=1 easy-k=3 hard-k=2
    show language.pencil-case-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.pencil-case-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.pencil-case-write k=0
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.pencil-case-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.pencil-case-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[school.do-you-have-a-pencil]] Do you have a pencil? [[school.yes-i-have]] Yes, I have. [[school.no-i-do-not-have]] No, I do not."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the pencil and the felt pen taken for each other, and a no answer
    heard as a yes because it starts with the same verb: every naming question offers the thing most
    often mixed up beside the right one. Each thing is drawn from the shelf's art tools, the same
    way every time, so the picture carries the answer to the naming questions and the child's job is
    the word. The step up from the animals lesson of grade two, which said I have a dog, is the
    question and the negative answer, so the child both asks and refuses. The listening question is
    a dictation: read the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or let the
    device play it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts there, while the words
    typed on their own in this lesson are taken without their accents. If the child is stuck, empty
    a real pencil case and name each thing as it comes out. Off the page, play a lending game at the
    table: one asks for a thing in the language and the other answers yes or no before handing it
    over. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7):
    "engage in conversations; ask and answer questions; express opinions and respond to those of
    others; seek clarification and help"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities:
    "Activities to ask and answer simple questions about each other's likes and wants, etc."
    (Japan's grade four book Let's Try 2 has a unit on asking for stationery, recalled); and CEFR
    A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases
    are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[school.the-felt-pen]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[school.the-pencil]] and [[school.the-crayon]] and the words to write are [[school.rubber]] and [[school.brush]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[school.the-brush]] and [[school.the-rubber]] and the words to write are [[school.felt-pen]], [[school.crayon]] and [[school.pencil]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
